/* Longer "Explain more" explanations, keyed by question id, with related equation-sheet entries. */
(function () {
"use strict";
const R = String.raw;
const D = {};
function d(id, text, eqs) { D[id] = { d: text, eq: eqs || [] }; }

/* ---------- Space environment ---------- */
d("env-1",R`Think of the four as "things in the environment that push on the spacecraft's attitude": photons (solar radiation pressure), the variation of gravity across a long body (gravity gradient), Earth's field acting on the spacecraft's residual magnetic dipole (magnetic), and air molecules hitting an off-center area (aerodynamic). Each one shows up as a curve on the torque-vs-altitude chart. Micrometeoroids and debris are real hazards, but they are random impacts, not a steady disturbance torque the ACS is designed around. That's why they are the odd one out.`,["solar","aerotorque"]);
d("env-2",R`Read the chart as "which curve is farthest right at a given altitude." Aerodynamic torque dies off exponentially because density does. Magnetic torque falls like \(1/r^3\) because Earth's dipole field does. Gravity-gradient torque also falls like \(1/r^3\). Solar radiation pressure depends on distance from the Sun, not from Earth, so it is the same at 300 km and at 36,000 km. Up at GEO, everything else has faded and SRP is what's left.`,["solar"]);
d("env-3",R`Drag torque is \(F_D(c_p-c_g)\), and \(F_D \propto \rho V^2\). At 200–400 km the density is still large enough (roughly \(10^{-10}\) to \(10^{-12}\) kg/m³) that this beats the other torques. That's why the aerodynamic curve crosses everything else at low altitude on the chart. A good exam answer pairs "dominant at low LEO" with "exponential density model."`,["drag","aerotorque","expatm"]);
d("env-4",R`MEO (about 2,000–35,000 km, period around 12 hr) sits right inside the Van Allen belts: the inner proton belt peaks near 1.5 Earth radii and the outer electron belt near 4–6. The air is far too thin for drag to matter, and the Sun is no stronger there than anywhere else near Earth, so trapped radiation dominates. This is why GPS satellites need radiation-hardened electronics and shielding.`,[]);
d("env-5",R`GEO is about 6.6 Earth radii out. That is above the dense part of the outer belt (the slide notes intensity is not maximum at GEO) and far above any atmosphere. What remains is the Sun: solar particle events, UV and thermal loading, and solar radiation pressure on large panels. Drag and atomic oxygen are LEO problems, and inner-belt protons sit much lower.`,["solar"]);
d("env-6",R`The 100 km figure comes from von Kármán's argument: below about 100 km, a vehicle can still fly on aerodynamic lift. Above it, you would need to go faster than orbital speed to get enough lift, so "flying" becomes "orbiting." 50 and 80 km are inside the mesosphere, and 200 km is an orbit altitude (though a short-lived one).`,[]);
d("env-7",R`1358 W/m² is the energy flux arriving per square meter facing the Sun at 1 AU. It sets solar-array power (\(\text{Power} = I_sA_\perp\) times cell efficiency). Don't mix it up with solar <i>pressure</i>: dividing by the speed of light gives \(1358/3\times10^8 \approx 4.5\times10^{-6}\ \mathrm{N/m^2}\), a force per area. About 1000 W/m² is what reaches the ground on a clear day after the atmosphere absorbs and scatters some, and 1580 W/m² is too high even at perihelion (the yearly maximum is about 1413).`,["solar"]);
d("env-8",R`Think about momentum. A black body absorbs the photon, so it picks up the photon's momentum once (\(q=0\), factor 1). A mirror reverses the photon, so the momentum change is twice as big (\(q=1\), factor 2). A transparent surface lets the photon pass untouched (\(q=-1\), factor 0). The \((1+q)\) term is just a way to write those three cases in one formula.`,["solar"]);
d("env-9",R`Flux spreads over a sphere of area \(4\pi r^2\), so it scales as \(1/r^2\). At 1.52 AU: \((1/1.52)^2 = 0.433\), so about 588 W/m² instead of 1358. The tempting wrong answer is 66% (that's \(1/1.52\), forgetting the square). This matters for sizing arrays for Mars or outer-planet missions, which is part of why Cassini used an RTG.`,["solar"]);
d("env-10",R`The derivation in the notes: ideal gas \(p = nkT\), and a force balance on a thin slab gives \(dp/dz = -nMg\). If \(T\) is constant, \(dp = kT\,dn\), so \(kT\,dn/dz = -nMg\), which gives \(dn/n = -dz/H\) with \(H = kT/(Mg)\). Integrating gives the exponential. If \(T\) changed with altitude, \(H\) would change too and the profile would no longer be a single exponential. That's why the real atmosphere is modeled as layers, each with its own \(H\).`,["expatm"]);
d("env-11",R`\(H\) has units of length: \(kT\) is energy (J), and \(Mg\) is weight per molecule (N), so J/N = m. Physically, it is how far you climb for density to fall by a factor of \(e \approx 2.72\). Hotter gas (larger \(T\)) means a larger \(H\) and density that falls off more slowly. That's the mechanism behind solar max raising drag at altitude.`,["expatm"]);
d("env-12",R`Drag deceleration is \(a_D = \tfrac12\rho V^2/\beta\). Same altitude means the same \(\rho\) and \(V\), so the only difference is \(1/\beta\). With \(\beta = 200\), deceleration is 10× smaller than with \(\beta = 20\), so the orbit decays about 10× more slowly. Intuitively, a dense brick coasts farther through air than a sheet of paper. On the lifetime chart, the higher-β curve sits at longer lifetimes for every altitude.`,["drag","decay"]);
d("env-13",R`On SMAD Fig. 9-19 (Midterm Review slide 6), the curves for all three ballistic coefficients drop below a ~10-year lifetime somewhere under 500 km. Since solar maxima come about every 11 years and puff up the thermosphere, anything below about 500 km will see at least one solar max and come down. Above about 600–700 km, lifetimes stretch to decades or centuries.`,["decay"]);
d("env-14",R`More solar EUV heats the thermosphere, which raises \(T\) and therefore the scale height \(H\). The whole density profile expands upward, so at a fixed altitude like 400 km you are now sitting in denser air. More density means more drag and faster decay. That's why each β on the lifetime chart has two curves, with the lower one being launch at solar max.`,["expatm","drag"]);
d("env-15",R`Below about 300 km, drag decays an orbit in days to weeks, so it dominates. Above about 600 km, decay takes years to centuries. In between it depends on β and the solar cycle, which is why the notes say drag is "worth considering for most all LEO altitudes depending on the mission type."`,["drag"]);
d("env-16",R`A force whose line of action passes through the center of gravity only translates a body. A force whose line of action misses the CG also rotates it. Drag acts at the center of pressure, which depends on the shape and areas facing the flow (solar panels, antennas). If \(c_p\) is behind \(c_g\), drag tends to weathervane the spacecraft. If it's off to the side, it produces a steady torque the ACS must fight. That's the HW 1c calculation: torque = drag force × 0.45 m offset.`,["aerotorque","drag"]);
d("env-17",R`The atmosphere rotates with Earth. At the equator the air moves east at about 0.46 km/s. A prograde satellite at about 7.7 km/s therefore sees roughly 7.2 km/s of relative wind. That relative velocity sets both the magnitude and direction of drag. Using the inertial velocity slightly overestimates drag for prograde orbits.`,["drag"]);
d("env-18",R`A good way to remember it: inner belt, heavy and hard (protons above 100 MeV, peak about 1.5 Earth radii); outer belt, light and wide (electrons 0.1–10 MeV, about 2–10 Earth radii). Protons cause displacement damage and single-event upsets. Electrons cause deep dielectric charging and total dose. GEO (6.6 Earth radii) sits in the outer electron belt's tail.`,[]);
d("env-19",R`Charged particles in a magnetic field spiral along field lines (gyroradius \(r_g = mv_\perp/|q|B\)) and bounce between mirror points near the poles, while drifting around Earth. Earth's dipole field traps energetic solar-wind and cosmic-ray particles this way, building two doughnut-shaped belts. Debris and atmospheric ionization don't produce trapped high-energy populations.`,["gyro"]);
d("env-20",R`Ionization needs both photons (to ionize) and enough molecules (to be ionized). Too high, there's too little gas. Too low, the UV has already been absorbed. The sweet spot is the F2 region, 200–400 km. It matters for spacecraft charging and for radio signals passing through, since electron density sets refraction and scintillation.`,[]);
d("env-21",R`The ionosphere's three problems are on Space Env slide 33. Atomic oxygen (from UV splitting O₂) chemically erodes polymers like Kapton. Free electrons bend and scatter radio signals. Surface charging happens when different surfaces collect different charge. Gravity gradient and coronal heating have nothing to do with the ionosphere.`,[]);
d("env-22",R`Charge only leaves a surface if it has a conductive path. An insulator (or a conductor not tied to structure) has none, so deposited electrons sit there "essentially forever in a perfect vacuum." A grounded conductor drains immediately. The danger is <i>differential</i> charging: neighboring surfaces at different potentials can arc and damage electronics. That's why designers ground everything they can.`,[]);
d("env-23",R`Keep the two kinds of radiation separate. <b>Particle</b> radiation is protons, electrons and ions, from three sources: the trapped belts, the Sun (wind and flares), and galactic cosmic rays from outside the solar system. <b>Electromagnetic</b> radiation (UV, IR, X-ray, gamma) comes mostly from the Sun. The wrong lists swap in the ionosphere (low-energy plasma, not energetic radiation) or Earth's albedo (reflected sunlight, which is electromagnetic, not particles).`,[]);
d("env-24",R`Satellites can't easily measure EUV from the ground, but the 10.7 cm radio flux tracks the same solar activity and has been measured daily since the 1940s. So it became the standard input to thermosphere density models (Jacchia and others). Higher F10.7 means a hotter, denser thermosphere and more drag. The ~11-year cycle in F10.7 is visible on slide 15.`,[]);
d("env-25",R`Order of names along the journey: meteor<b>oid</b> while in space, meteor (the streak of light) while in the atmosphere, meteor<b>ite</b> once it hits the ground. "Bolide" is a very bright fireball meteor, not part of the course's three-term list.`,[]);
d("env-26",R`A thick single wall gets punched through by a hypervelocity particle (around 10 km/s). A Whipple shield puts a thin bumper in front. The impactor shatters or vaporizes on the bumper, and the spread-out cloud spreads its energy over a larger area of the back wall. "Stuffed" means extra layers (like Kevlar or Nextel) fill the gap. It's the same idea as spaced armor.`,[]);

/* ---------- Orbital mechanics ---------- */
d("orb-27",R`Use \(\varepsilon = -\mu/2a\). For positive \(\varepsilon\), \(a\) must be negative, which is how a hyperbola's semi-major axis is defined. Positive energy means kinetic energy beats potential energy, so the craft reaches infinity with speed left over, \(v_\infty = \sqrt{2\varepsilon}\). Then from \(e = \sqrt{1+2\varepsilon H^2/\mu^2}\), \(\varepsilon > 0\) gives \(e > 1\). All of the table's columns come from those two relations.`,["energy","traj"]);
d("orb-28",R`At \(\varepsilon = 0\), \(v^2/2 = \mu/r\): kinetic exactly equals the magnitude of potential. That's the minimum energy to escape, arriving at infinity with zero speed. For a circle, \(v^2 = \mu/r\), so \(KE = \tfrac12|PE|\), and \(|PE| = 2KE\) holds only for the circle. That's why "\(|PE| = 2|KE|\)" is a distractor.`,["energy","vcirc"]);
d("orb-29",R`One expression is the definition of specific energy at any point (kinetic plus potential). The other is what that energy equals in terms of orbit size. Equate them and solve for \(v\): \(v^2 = 2\mu/r - \mu/a\). Remembering this derivation means you never need to memorize vis-viva separately, and you can get the circular and escape special cases instantly.`,["visviva","energy"]);
d("orb-30",R`Vis-viva needs the orbit's \(a\), and for an ellipse \(a\) is the average of the perigee and apogee radii. Then plug in the radius where you want the speed. The circular formula \(\sqrt{\mu/r_p}\) is wrong because the ellipse isn't circular: at perigee you're moving <i>faster</i> than circular, which is exactly why the orbit swings out to \(r_a\). Check: 9.6 km/s at 8000 km, vs 7.05 km/s circular.`,["visviva","geom"]);
d("orb-31",R`Escape means \(\varepsilon = 0\), so \(-\mu/2a = 0\), which needs \(a \to \infty\). With \(1/a = 0\), vis-viva gives \(\sqrt{2\mu/r}\). Setting \(r \to \infty\) gives \(v \to \sqrt{-\mu/a}\), which isn't the escape case.`,["vcirc","visviva"]);
d("orb-32",R`\(v_c^2 = \mu/r\) and \(v_{esc}^2 = 2\mu/r\), so \(v_{esc}/v_c = \sqrt2\). The extra 41% isn't small. From LEO it's about 3.2 km/s, the "3210 to escape" leg on the subway map. Useful exam check: if your hyperbolic burnout speed isn't above \(\sqrt2\,v_c\), you haven't escaped.`,["vcirc","c3"]);
d("orb-33",R`Kepler's third law says only size matters for period. A circle of radius \(a\) and a very skinny ellipse with semi-major axis \(a\) take the same time. That's why the Hohmann TOF only needs \(a_{tr}\). It's also why the GEO radius (42,164 km) is fixed by requiring a 1-sidereal-day period. Eccentricity, perigee alone, and inclination don't change it.`,["period"]);
d("orb-34",R`Every orbit formula measures \(r\) from the planet's center, because gravity acts from the center. Plugging \(r = 400\) km into \(\sqrt{\mu/r}\) gives 31.6 km/s, which is absurd. Quick sanity anchors: LEO \(v_c \approx 7.7\) km/s, GEO \(\approx 3.07\) km/s. If your number is wildly off those, check \(r = R_\oplus + h\) first.`,["vcirc"]);
d("orb-35",R`The two-body problem allows any conic: circle, ellipse, parabola or hyperbola. The trajectory equation produces all of them. The actual assumptions are about the bodies and forces: spherical symmetry (so they act like point masses), no other forces, and (for the restricted problem) \(M \gg m\) so the big body doesn't move.`,["eom"]);
d("orb-36",R`Torque about the central body is \(\vec r\times\vec F\), and gravity points along \(\vec r\), so the torque is zero and \(\vec H\) is constant. Since \(\vec H = \vec r\times\vec v\) is perpendicular to both \(\vec r\) and \(\vec v\), they must stay in the fixed plane perpendicular to \(\vec H\). Constant energy controls the size of the orbit, not its plane.`,["angmom","eom"]);
d("orb-37",R`\(H = |\vec r\times\vec v| = rv\sin(\text{angle between } \vec r \text{ and } \vec v)\). Measuring from the local horizontal instead, that angle is \(90°-\phi\), so \(H = rv\cos\phi\). At periapsis and apoapsis the velocity is purely horizontal, so \(\phi = 0\) and \(H = rv\). That's why \(r_pv_p = r_av_a\), a handy check on vis-viva answers.`,["angmom"]);
d("orb-38",R`At \(\theta = 90°\), \(\cos\theta = 0\) so \(r = p\): the parameter is the radius straight "above" the focus. It connects the dynamics (\(p = H^2/\mu\)) to the geometry (\(p = a(1-e^2)\)). In HW 2 #1 you compute \(H\) from \(r\) and \(v\), then \(p\), then \(e\).`,["traj","geom"]);
d("orb-39",R`Subtract \(r_p = a(1-e)\) from \(r_a = a(1+e)\): \(r_a - r_p = 2ae\). Add them: \(r_a + r_p = 2a\). Divide to get \(e = (r_a-r_p)/(r_a+r_p)\). Check with a circle: \(r_a = r_p\) gives \(e = 0\). The distractor \(1 - r_p/r_a\) looks plausible but gives the wrong number for most ellipses.`,["geom"]);
d("orb-40",R`With no torque, \(H = r v_\perp\) is constant, so where \(r\) is smallest \(v_\perp\) must be largest. Energy tells the same story: at small \(r\), potential energy is most negative, so kinetic energy is largest. Drag and thrusters aren't part of the two-body picture.`,["angmom","visviva"]);
d("orb-41",R`Bracket the given speed. At \(r = 6871\) km, \(v_c = 7.62\) and \(v_{esc} = 10.77\) km/s. 10.4 is between them, so the orbit is bound (\(\varepsilon < 0\)) but not circular. Since the velocity is perpendicular to \(r\), this point is an apse, and since \(v > v_c\) it is perigee (the orbit rises from here). Faster than circular does not mean hyperbolic.`,["vcirc","energy"]);
d("orb-42",R`\(\mu = GM\) is known far more precisely than \(G\) or \(M\) separately, so orbit work uses \(\mu\) directly. Units matter: 398,600 pairs with km³/s², so it goes with km and km/s. In m³/s² the same value is \(3.986\times10^{14}\), so \"398,600 m³/s²\" and \"\(3.986\times10^{14}\) km³/s²\" mix the two up. \(1.327\times10^{11}\) km³/s² is the Sun's \(\mu\).`,["eom"]);
d("orb-43",R`Kepler found \(P^2 \propto a^3\) empirically in 1619. Newton later derived the constant, \(4\pi^2/\mu\), giving \(P = 2\pi\sqrt{a^3/\mu}\). A quick check rules out the others: doubling \(a\) multiplies \(P\) by \(2^{3/2} \approx 2.83\), not 8 (\(P \propto a^3\)), and the law uses \(a\), not \(r_p\).`,["period"]);
d("orb-44",R`\(\varepsilon\) and \(P\) depend only on \(a\), so both orbits match. Angular momentum differs: \(H = \sqrt{\mu a(1-e^2)}\) is largest for the circle and shrinks as \(e\) grows. A very eccentric orbit is "mostly radial" motion.`,["energy","period","angmom"]);

/* ---------- Transfers ---------- */
d("man-45",R`Burn 1 changes your orbit from the circle to the transfer ellipse at the same point. Before the burn you have \(v_{c1}\). After it you need the ellipse's perigee speed, \(v_p = \sqrt{\mu(2/r_1 - 1/a_{tr})}\). The difference is the burn. Common slips: using \(r_2\) in place of \(a_{tr}\) (that's the wrong ellipse), evaluating at \(r_2\) instead of \(r_1\) (that's the apogee speed), or using \(v_{c2}-v_{c1}\), which is negative for orbit raising and is the Edelbaum spiral result, not a Hohmann burn.`,["hohmann","visviva"]);
d("man-46",R`Each burn happens at a different place and speed, and real missions modify them. You might combine burn 2 with a plane change (HW 2 Transfer II), split a burn between stages, or use different engines with different Isp. You can only do that if you have \(v_{c1}, v_p, v_a, v_{c2}\) as separate numbers. A single lumped formula gives one number and hides all the pieces you need for mission analysis.`,["hohmann"]);
d("man-47",R`The transfer goes from perigee to apogee, which is half an ellipse. By symmetry (Kepler's 2nd law), that takes half the period. So TOF \(= \tfrac12\cdot2\pi\sqrt{a_{tr}^3/\mu} = \pi\sqrt{a_{tr}^3/\mu}\). Use \(a_{tr}\), not \(r_1\) or \(r_2\), and use \(\pi\) (half a period), not \(2\pi\).`,["hohmann","period"]);
d("man-48",R`Edelbaum assumes the thrust is small and continuous, so the orbit stays nearly circular while slowly spiraling out. Thrust is spread over the whole path, and plane change is mixed in gradually (hence the \(\pi/2\) factor). It is not valid for impulsive burns, escape, or launch.`,["edelbaum"]);
d("man-49",R`With \(\Delta\alpha = 0\) the root becomes \(\sqrt{(v_{c1}-v_{c2})^2}\). Physically, slow continuous thrust makes the spacecraft spiral out, and the total Δv is just the difference in circular speeds. It's larger than Hohmann because thrust is applied when it's less efficient, losing the Oberth benefit of a big burn at perigee.`,["edelbaum"]);
d("man-50",R`The argument of cosine must be in radians for the \(\pi/2\) scaling to mean anything: the formula maps \(\Delta\alpha\) in radians into an angle \(\tfrac{\pi}{2}\Delta\alpha\). Plug 28.5 (degrees) in by mistake and you take the cosine of 44.8 rad, which is nonsense. Convert first: \(28.5° = 0.497\) rad.`,["edelbaum"]);
d("man-51",R`Propellant depends on \(\Delta V/c\) exponentially. Hohmann LEO→GEO: about 3.9 km/s at \(c \approx 3\) km/s gives \(m_p/m_f \approx 2.7\). Spiral: about 4.7 km/s at \(c \approx 30\) km/s gives \(m_p/m_f \approx 0.17\). That's roughly 15× less propellant despite more Δv. The cost is time (about 200 days instead of 5 hours), power hardware, and time spent in the Van Allen belts.`,["edelbaum","rocket"]);
d("man-52",R`Draw the two velocity vectors, same length \(v\), with angle \(\Delta\alpha\) between them. The Δv vector connects their tips, forming an isosceles triangle. Split it in half and \(\Delta v/2 = v\sin(\Delta\alpha/2)\). For small angles, \(2\sin(x/2) \approx x\) (radians), so \(v\Delta\alpha\) is the small-angle approximation, not exact.`,["planechange"]);
d("man-53",R`\(\Delta v = 2v\sin(\Delta\alpha/2)\) is proportional to \(v\). At GEO, \(v \approx 3.07\) km/s vs 7.7 km/s in LEO, so the same plane change is 2.5× cheaper. The Oberth effect argues for burning at high speed to change <i>energy</i>, but a plane change rotates the velocity without changing energy, so slower is better.`,["planechange"]);
d("man-54",R`1° in LEO: \(2(7.7)\sin(0.5°) = 0.134\) km/s. Raising 100 km: about 0.05 km/s from Hohmann math. So 1° costs about 2.5× as much as a 100 km raise. The chart's "bottom line: plane change is expensive" is why missions pick launch sites and azimuths to get the right inclination from the start.`,["planechange","hohmann"]);
d("man-55",R`If both \(i\) and \(\Omega\) change, the planes are rotated about a tilted axis, and the actual angle between them is not simply \(|i_2-i_1|\). The spherical-trig formula gives the true \(\Delta\alpha\), which then goes into the plane-change Δv formula.`,["sphtrig","planechange"]);
d("man-56",R`Two separate burns cost \(|\Delta\vec v_a| + |\Delta\vec v_b|\). One combined burn costs \(|\Delta\vec v_a + \Delta\vec v_b|\), and the triangle inequality says that's never more. At GEO apogee you have \(v_a \approx 1.6\) km/s and need \(v_{c2} \approx 3.07\) km/s rotated by 28°. Doing it in one shot with the law of cosines saves a few hundred m/s.`,["planechange","hohmann"]);
d("man-57",R`Propellant flows at \(\dot m\), so burning \(m_p\) takes \(m_p/\dot m\). Since \(F = \dot m c\), \(\dot m = F/c\), giving TOF \(= c\,m_p/F\). High \(c\) with tiny \(F\) means long TOF, which is the EP trade.`,["edelbaum","thrust"]);
d("man-58",R`Hohmann TOF \(= \tfrac12P_{tr}\) with \(a_{tr} \approx 24{,}400\) km, about 5.3 hr. A spiral at millinewtons of thrust takes months to accumulate km/s of Δv. These two numbers frame the Chem vs EP decision on the propulsion slides.`,["hohmann","edelbaum"]);

/* ---------- 3D & coverage ---------- */
d("cov-59",R`RAAN is measured in the equatorial plane, from the vernal equinox direction to where the orbit crosses the equator going north. It sets which way the orbit plane "faces." \(\omega\) is measured in the orbit plane from the node to perigee, \(i\) is the tilt, and \(\theta\) is the satellite's position from perigee.`,["sphtrig"]);
d("cov-60",R`\(a\) and \(e\) are the ellipse's size and shape, and \(\theta\) is where the satellite is on it. Those three describe everything you'd see if you drew the orbit flat on paper. \(i\), \(\Omega\) and \(\omega\) are the three angles that place that paper in 3D space. The Orbit Angle Lab's orbital elements tab shows this split.`,[]);
d("cov-61",R`Memorize the boundaries: LEO up to about 1200 km (below the inner belt's worst), GEO at 35,786 km (the altitude giving a 1-sidereal-day period), MEO everything between. Remember these are altitudes. The GEO <i>radius</i> is 42,164 km, a common mix-up.`,["period"]);
d("cov-62",R`From \(P = 2\pi\sqrt{a^3/\mu}\): at 400 km, about 92 min. GPS at 20,200 km, about 12 hr (half a day, so ground tracks repeat daily). GEO, one sidereal day (23 h 56 m). You can check any of these with the period formula.`,["period"]);
d("cov-63",R`GPS needs at least 4 satellites in view everywhere (3 for position plus 1 for clock bias). Six planes × 4 satellites at 55° and 20,200 km does that. Iridium is the LEO comms constellation, and "3 in GEO" is the classic global-comms coverage example.`,[]);
d("cov-64",R`Iridium was originally designed with 77 satellites, the atomic number of iridium. The design was reduced to 66 (which would be dysprosium) but kept the name. At about 780 km, each satellite covers a small footprint, so many are needed for global coverage.`,[]);
d("cov-65",R`"Synchronous" means the period matches Earth's rotation. "Stationary" adds that it also doesn't move relative to the ground. That needs \(i = 0\) (so it doesn't bob north–south) and \(e = 0\) (so it doesn't drift east–west during the day). An inclined GSO traces a figure-8 on the ground track.`,[]);
d("cov-66",R`The minimum elevation angle is how high above the ground station's horizon the satellite must be to be usable (buildings, terrain, atmosphere). At \(\varepsilon = 0\) you get the full horizon-to-horizon view. As \(\varepsilon\) increases, \(\lambda\) shrinks. Don't confuse it with eccentricity \(e\) or energy \(\varepsilon\), which share the symbol.`,["coverage"]);
d("cov-67",R`\(A_t = 2\pi R_E^2(1-\cos\lambda)\) is the area of a spherical cap of half-angle \(\lambda\). Dividing by the sphere's area \(4\pi R_E^2\) gives \(F\). Example: GEO with \(\varepsilon = 0\) has \(\lambda \approx 81°\), so \(F \approx 42\%\) of the Earth. That's why 3 GEO satellites can cover nearly everything except the poles.`,["coverage"]);
d("cov-68",R`A higher \(\varepsilon\) requirement throws away the edge of the visible region, where the satellite is low on the horizon. So \(\lambda\) (central angle) decreases, and so do area and fraction. It also shortens each pass duration, which you used in HW 2 #6.`,["coverage"]);
d("cov-69",R`The Sun appears to move east about 0.9856°/day. To keep the orbit plane at the same angle to the Sun, \(\Omega\) must advance east at the same rate, so \(\dot\Omega > 0\). The formula has a leading minus sign, so you need \(\cos i < 0\), meaning \(i > 90°\). For typical LEO altitudes that lands around 96°–100°.`,["j2node","sso"]);
d("cov-70",R`360° in 365.256 days (one year, sidereal) is 0.9856°/day, positive (eastward). A negative rate would drift the plane the wrong way relative to the Sun.`,["sso","j2node"]);
d("cov-71",R`\(\dot\omega \propto (2 - \tfrac52\sin^2 i)\). Set it to zero: \(\sin^2 i = 4/5\), so \(\sin i = 0.894\) and \(i = 63.4°\) or \(180° - 63.4° = 116.6°\). Molniya orbits use 63.4° so their high apogee stays over Russia permanently.`,["j2apse"]);
d("cov-72",R`Earth bulges about 21 km at the equator. That extra ring of mass pulls on an inclined orbit, creating a torque that makes the orbit plane precess (nodal regression) and the ellipse rotate in its plane (apsidal rotation). J₂ is the coefficient that measures this bulge.`,["j2node","j2apse"]);
d("cov-73",R`\(\cos 90° = 0\). Physically, the bulge's torque on a polar orbit is symmetric and cancels, so the plane doesn't precess. Low-inclination LEO orbits precess fastest (up to about 10°/day).`,["j2node"]);
d("cov-74",R`Two-body assumes a point-mass Earth and no other forces. Real deviations: Earth isn't a sphere (J₂ etc.), the Moon and Sun pull (third body), air drags (drag), and sunlight pushes (SRP). Kepler's laws and launch azimuth aren't perturbations.`,["j2node","drag","solar"]);
d("cov-75",R`Stars are effectively at infinity and don't move against the celestial frame, so a telescope's targets are described in celestial coordinates (right ascension and declination). The spacecraft-fixed frame describes the instrument and boresight. Earth-fixed coordinates rotate with the planet, which is useless for pointing at stars.`,[]);
d("cov-76",R`6371 km is the mean radius used in this course's homework. The equatorial value, 6378 km, appears in J₂ formulas because J₂ is defined about the equatorial bulge. Mixing them up changes answers by about 0.1%, which is fine for estimates but worth being consistent.`,["j2node"]);
d("cov-77",R`A parking orbit is a waiting spot (checkout, phasing, waiting for the right moment to burn). A transfer orbit is the path between orbits, such as GTO's 320 × 35,786 km ellipse, which is the first half of a Hohmann to GEO.`,["hohmann"]);

/* ---------- Rocket basics ---------- */
d("rkt-78",R`Pressure thrust is \(A_e(P_e - P_\infty)\). It's zero only if exit pressure equals ambient (perfect expansion). In vacuum, \(P_\infty = 0\), so the term is \(A_eP_e > 0\), positive and not zero. That's why vacuum nozzles (like Merlin Vacuum) are large: they expand further to turn that pressure into velocity.`,["thrust"]);
d("rkt-79",R`Isp was historically defined as thrust divided by propellant <i>weight</i> flow rate, with weight measured on Earth. Using \(g_0 = 9.81\) m/s² as a fixed conversion makes Isp a standard number anywhere. The local gravity on Mars has nothing to do with the engine. Convert Isp to \(c\) immediately and work in \(c\).`,["isp"]);
d("rkt-80",R`Isp tells you how much impulse (thrust × time) you get from each unit weight of propellant. Higher Isp means more Δv from the same propellant. It says nothing about thrust level: an ion engine has huge Isp but tiny thrust.`,["isp"]);
d("rkt-81",R`Chemical rockets are limited by chemical energy, topping out around 450 s (LOX/LH₂). Electrothermal (arcjets, resistojets) heats propellant electrically, about 300–1200 s. Electrostatic and EM thrusters accelerate ions directly, about 1500–4000 s or more.`,["isp"]);
d("rkt-82",R`The derivation balances only thrust against changing momentum. No gravity, no drag. That's why launch Δv budgets subtract gravity and drag losses separately. Constant \(c\) lets you pull it out of the integral. Mass is definitely not constant (that's the whole point).`,["rocket"]);
d("rkt-83",R`Start from \(m_0/m_f = e^{\Delta V/c}\). Since \(m_0 = m_f + m_p\), \(m_p/m_f = e^{\Delta V/c} - 1\). This form is the fastest when you know the dry mass, which is the usual mission question ("how much propellant to deliver X kg"). The \(m_0(1-e^{-\Delta V/c})\) form is equivalent but needs the wet mass.`,["rocket"]);
d("rkt-84",R`Rocket equation: \(m_p = m_f(e^{\Delta V/c}-1)\). Any extra dry mass \(m_f\) multiplies the propellant needed, which compounds. Every listed method either increases \(c\) (Isp), decreases \(\Delta V\) (trajectory, flyby), drops mass along the way (staging), or decreases \(m_f\).`,["rocket"]);
d("rkt-85",R`\(m_f\) is everything left when propellant is gone: payload plus inert mass (tanks, engine, structure, power, avionics). It's what gets "delivered." Using payload alone underestimates propellant, since inert mass must be accelerated too.`,["rocket"]);
d("rkt-86",R`\(m_p/m_f = e^{\Delta V/c}-1\). Once \(\Delta V/c\) is small (say 5 km/s at 44 km/s), \(e^x - 1 \approx x\), about 0.12. Doubling Isp only halves an already small number, while the power system needed (\(P \propto Fc\)) grows. That's why about 4500 s is the practical sweet spot on the slide.`,["rocket","epeff"]);
d("rkt-87",R`\(c = I_{sp}g\): cold gas 70 s → 0.69 km/s, hydrazine 230 s → 2.26, solid 300 s → 2.94, biprop 400 s → 3.92. You can regenerate these from Isp, so remember Isp and multiply.`,["isp"]);
d("rkt-88",R`From stage 1's perspective, stage 2 and the payload are just "payload." So \(m_{0,2}\) is all of stage 2 (propellant, inert) plus the payload. Stage 1's final mass \(m_{f,1}\) is \(m_{0,2}\) plus stage 1's empty inert mass.`,["staging"]);
d("rkt-89",R`ΔV per stage uses \(c\) and the mass ratio. Throttling all engines equally changes thrust and mass flow but not the flow-weighted Isp, and drops no hardware, so the rocket equation for that segment is unchanged. Just keep integrating the same stage.`,["staging","ispmix"]);
d("rkt-90",R`Before burnout, the stage Isp is the SRB-and-core blend (e.g., 289 s). After burnout, only the core fires (e.g., 500 s), so a different \(c\) applies. You must split the rocket equation at that point. When the SRBs then drop, dry mass changes, another split.`,["staging","ispmix"]);
d("rkt-91",R`Isp for a cluster is \(\sum F / \sum(\dot m g)\). Each engine's thrust is \(\dot m_j I_{sp,j} g\), so this is a mass-flow-weighted average. The SRBs dominate the flow (8000 vs 1475 kg/s), so the stage Isp is close to 250 s, not 375.`,["ispmix"]);
d("rkt-92",R`A single-stage rocket hauls its empty tanks all the way to orbit. Staging throws away dead weight once its propellant is used, improving the mass ratio for later burns. Each stage can also have an engine optimized for its altitude.`,["staging"]);
d("rkt-93",R`About 90% propellant is typical. With \(\Delta V \approx 9.4\) km/s and \(c \approx 3\) km/s, \(m_0/m_f = e^{3.1} \approx 22\), so about 95% would be propellant in a single stage. Staging and better Isp bring the average to about 90%.`,["rocket"]);
d("rkt-94",R`Parallel: strap-on boosters and core light together at liftoff (more thrust early), then boosters drop. Serial: one stage burns out, drops, then the next lights. Falcon Heavy and Delta IV Heavy combine both (boosters plus core, then an upper stage).`,["staging"]);
d("rkt-95",R`Impulse is force × time, the momentum delivered: N·s. Total impulse needed is \(\int F\,dt\). For a fixed total impulse, Isp determines how much propellant weight it costs: \(W_p = I/I_{sp}\).`,["isp"]);
d("rkt-96",R`9.5 km/s to LEO includes about 1.5–2 km/s of losses on top of the about 7.8 km/s orbital speed. Then about 4.2 km/s more takes you from a 30° LEO parking orbit to GEO (Hohmann plus plane change). These two numbers are the classic rough budget.`,["launchdv","hohmann"]);

/* ---------- Chemical ---------- */
d("chem-97",R`\(V_e^2 \propto T_c/\bar M\): hot and light. Hot is limited by chemistry and materials. Light is why hydrogen-rich exhaust (H₂O, H₂) performs well. Chamber pressure affects nozzle expansion, but not this ideal-velocity relation directly.`,["ve"]);
d("chem-98",R`\(c^* = P_cA_t/\dot m\) tells you how much chamber pressure you get per unit mass flow through a given throat, so it depends only on combustion (temperature, molecular weight, \(\gamma\)). The nozzle comes in through \(c_f\). Splitting them lets engineers diagnose which part is underperforming.`,["cstar"]);
d("chem-99",R`\(c = c^*c_f\). The chamber is unchanged, so \(c^*\) is unchanged, and \(c\) goes up by exactly the factor \(c_f\) did. Then propellant changes through the rocket equation, so the % mass savings is <i>not</i> 3% (it depends on \(\Delta V/c\)). HW 3 asks you to compute that.`,["cstar","rocket"]);
d("chem-100",R`Choked flow means the throat flow is sonic (M = 1). Then downstream conditions can't influence upstream, and \(\dot m\) depends only on \(P_c\), \(T_c\), \(A_t\) and \(\gamma\). That's why \(\dot m \propto P_cA_t/\sqrt{T_c}\).`,["choked"]);
d("chem-101",R`Know the hierarchy: cold gas (just pressurized gas), liquid (monoprop: one propellant over a catalyst; biprop: fuel plus oxidizer), solid (premixed grain), hybrid (solid fuel plus liquid oxidizer). Electric thrusters are a separate family.`,[]);
d("chem-102",R`Cold gas has no combustion, so it's simple, cheap, safe and clean (good near optics). But exhaust velocity is limited to gas expansion, about 0.7 km/s (Isp about 70 s). Used for small attitude control, not big Δv.`,[]);
d("chem-103",R`Biprop: control (throttle, restart, shutdown) and the highest chemical Isp, at the cost of pumps or pressurization, valves, possible leaks, and cryogenic boil-off. Solid motors: simple and storable, but once lit they burn to completion.`,[]);
d("chem-104",R`SRMs have a dense, premixed propellant in a single casing: no plumbing, compact, storable for years (good for missiles and boosters). Downsides: they can't be throttled (thrust is set by grain shape), restart is limited, and many produce toxic exhaust (HCl from AP-based propellants).`,["regress"]);
d("chem-105",R`Higher chamber pressure pushes more heat into the grain surface, so it burns faster. \(r = aP_c^n\) captures this. If \(n\) is too close to 1, burning becomes unstable (more pressure, faster burn, more pressure). Grain geometry controls burning <i>area</i>, a separate effect.`,["regress"]);
d("chem-106",R`Hybrids keep fuel and oxidizer in different phases. That makes them safer (they can't mix accidentally) and restartable (shut the oxidizer valve), but the mixture ratio drifts as the fuel port grows, which hurts efficiency.`,[]);
d("chem-107",R`Cut a sphere in half. Pressure force on the cut, \(P\pi r^2\), is balanced by wall stress on the rim, \(\sigma\cdot2\pi rt\). So \(\sigma = Pr/2t\). A cylinder's hoop direction sees double that (\(Pr/t\)), which is why spherical tanks are mass-efficient.`,["tank"]);
d("chem-108",R`Hydrazine decomposes exothermically over an iridium catalyst bed (Shell 405), producing hot N₂, H₂ and NH₃. No oxidizer is needed, so it's simple and reliable. Isp is about 220–235 s. It's the default for spacecraft attitude control.`,[]);

/* ---------- Electric propulsion ---------- */
d("ep-109",R`An ion of charge \(q\) falling through a potential drop \(V_B\) gains energy \(qV_B\) (like a ball rolling downhill). Starting from rest, \(\tfrac12mv^2 = qV_B\), so \(v = \sqrt{2qV_B/m}\). Momentum conservation describes the reaction on the spacecraft, and \(v\times B\) doesn't do work.`,["vplus","lorentz"]);
d("ep-110",R`\(F_T = \dot m_p\eta_m v_+\) and \(v_+ = \sqrt{2eV_B/m}\), so at fixed \(\dot m\), \(F \propto \sqrt{V_B}\). Quadrupling the voltage doubles the thrust (and Isp), but quadruples beam power, which is why EP is power-limited.`,["vplus","epperf"]);
d("ep-111",R`Not every propellant atom gets ionized. Neutrals leak out slowly, adding mass flow but almost no thrust. \(\eta_m = \dot m_i/\dot m_p\) is the useful fraction, typically 0.8–0.95.`,["epperf"]);
d("ep-112",R`Current is charge per second. Ion mass flow \(\dot m_i\) divided by mass per ion \(m\) gives ions per second. Times charge per ion \(q\) gives coulombs per second (amps). And \(\dot m_i = \eta_m\dot m_p\).`,["epperf"]);
d("ep-113",R`Beam power \(J_BV_B\) is what actually accelerates ions. The rest, \(P_0\), goes to ionizing the gas, heating cathodes, and losses. \(\eta_E\) is the electrical fraction that does useful work.`,["epeff"]);
d("ep-114",R`Jet power is \(\tfrac12\dot m c^2\), the kinetic energy rate of the exhaust. Divided by input electrical power, it's the overall efficiency. It combines mass, electrical and other losses.`,["epeff"]);
d("ep-115",R`For EP, \(P_{jet} = \tfrac12\dot mc^2 = \tfrac12Fc\). With limited power, thrust \(F = 2\eta_TP_E/c\) drops as \(c\) rises. Chemical rockets carry their energy in the propellant, which caps \(c\) but allows huge thrust.`,["epeff"]);
d("ep-116",R`EP needs solar arrays, PPUs and radiators, so inert mass grows. That's the main disadvantage alongside low thrust. It saves propellant, so it has high Isp. It's precise and has low disturbance (those are advantages).`,[]);
d("ep-117",R`\(q\vec v\times\vec B\) is perpendicular to motion. It doesn't add energy but bends trajectories into spirals, which is the basis for magnetic confinement (Hall thruster electrons, Van Allen belts) and for EM thrusters where \(\vec J\times\vec B\) pushes plasma.`,["lorentz","gyro"]);
d("ep-118",R`\(v_+ \propto 1/\sqrt{m}\). Lighter krypton gives \(\sqrt{131.3/83.8} = 1.25\) times faster ions, so higher Isp. But for the same mass flow, thrust \(\propto 1/\sqrt m\) and current \(\propto 1/m\), so more power is needed. Xenon also ionizes more easily.`,["vplus"]);
d("ep-119",R`The Hall thruster's electric field comes from electrons trapped in the radial magnetic field, creating the azimuthal Hall current and high resistance. If ions were magnetized too, they'd spiral instead of accelerating straight out. \(r_g \propto m\) makes this possible.`,["gyro"]);
d("ep-120",R`Compute: \(m = 131.3\times1.66\times10^{-27}\). Then \(v = \sqrt{2(1.6\times10^{-19})(1000)/m} = 38.3\) km/s, and \(I_{sp} = v/g = 3906\) s. Good to remember as an order-of-magnitude anchor for ion engines.`,["vplus"]);
d("ep-121",R`SMART-1 (2003) used a Hall thruster to spiral from GTO all the way to lunar orbit over about 14 months, including plane changes. It was the first Hall thruster used for primary propulsion beyond GEO.`,["edelbaum"]);
d("ep-122",R`Electrostatic (gridded ion, Hall) gives the high Isp and is mature. Electrothermal (resistojet, arcjet) is basically "better chemical." Electromagnetic (MPD, PPT) is promising but considered under development for mission design.`,[]);
d("ep-123",R`Xenon is scarce and expensive. Thousands of Starlink satellites need cheaper propellant, so krypton (Gen 1) and argon (Gen 2) trade some efficiency for cost. Lighter atoms give higher Isp but need more power per newton.`,["vplus"]);

/* ---------- Launch ---------- */
d("lau-124",R`At \(A = 90°\), \(\sin A = 1\), so \(\cos i = \cos\delta\) and \(i = \delta\). Any other azimuth makes \(\sin A < 1\), so \(\cos i\) is smaller and \(i\) is larger. That's why the launch latitude is the minimum inclination you can reach directly, and why \(i \ge \delta\) is a rule you can quote on an exam. The Orbit Angle Lab's launch azimuth tab lets you sweep \(A\) and watch \(i\) change.`,["azimuth"]);
d("lau-125",R`\(\cos i = \cos\delta\sin A \le \cos\delta\), so \(i \ge \delta = 28.5°\) from KSC. Launching due south doesn't help: it gives a polar-ish orbit. To get to 0°, you have to do a plane change in orbit, which costs \(2v\sin(\Delta\alpha/2)\). That's cheapest at GTO apogee, which is how GEO satellites from Florida do it. HW 4 #1 makes you compute this penalty per spaceport. French Guiana (5°) pays almost nothing.`,["azimuth","planechange"]);
d("lau-126",R`The ground at latitude \(\delta\) moves east at \(\omega_\oplus R_\oplus\cos\delta\): about 0.465 km/s at the equator and 0.41 km/s at KSC. The \(\sin A\) factor projects that onto your launch direction. Due east gets all of it, and a westward (retrograde) launch has \(\sin A < 0\), so you pay it back. Polar launches (\(A \approx 180°\)) get almost nothing.`,["launchdv","azimuth"]);
d("lau-127",R`Read it as a velocity budget. The rocket equation supplies \(\Delta V_{prop}\). Gravity loss (\(\int g\sin\gamma\,dt\)) is what you spend holding yourself up while climbing. Drag loss (\(\int D/m\,dt\)) is air resistance. Steering loss (\(\int (T/m)(1-\cos\alpha)\,dt\)) is thrust pointed off your velocity vector. Earth's rotation adds a free boost. What remains is orbital speed. Shuttle: 9086 − 1222 − 107 − 358 + 395 ≈ 7794 m/s.`,["launchdv","rocket"]);
d("lau-128",R`Two competing losses. Drag (\(\tfrac12\rho V^2C_DA\)) is huge low down where \(\rho\) is high, so you want to get through the thick air quickly, which means going straight up. Gravity loss (\(\int g\sin\gamma\,dt\)) is largest when flying vertically (\(\gamma = 90°\)), so once the air is thin you pitch over toward horizontal (\(\gamma \to 0\)) and put thrust into orbital speed. A gravity turn lets gravity itself bend the trajectory over smoothly, keeping the angle of attack (and steering loss) near zero.`,["launchdv","drag"]);
d("lau-129",R`Gravity loss is 1222 m/s, more than 10× drag (107 m/s). A rocket spends several minutes climbing with a large \(\sin\gamma\), and every second of that, part of its thrust only cancels gravity. Drag is concentrated in a short window around Max Q. This is the main reason high thrust-to-weight early in flight matters: the faster you get up and over, the less gravity loss.`,["launchdv"]);
d("lau-130",R`About 40:1 to LEO and 160:1 to GEO. These ratios come straight from the rocket equation with realistic Isp, staging and losses (about 9.4 km/s to LEO, plus about 4 km/s more to GEO). They're a good gut check: if a calculation says 10 kg of propellant puts 4.5 kg in LEO, something's wrong.`,["rocket","launchdv"]);
d("lau-131",R`Range safety won't let a rocket fly over populated land in case it fails. From KSC, the allowed azimuths (about 35°–120°) point over the Atlantic, giving \(i\) ≈ 28.5°–57°. Vandenberg on the California coast can launch south over the Pacific (about 140°–201°), reaching polar and retrograde orbits (sun-synchronous at about 98°). Together they cover the full range, which is the point of the "Need both launch sites" note.`,["azimuth"]);
d("lau-132",R`The target orbit plane is fixed in inertial space (set by \(\Omega\) and \(i\)), but the launch site rotates with Earth once a day. So there are only certain moments when the site sits under the desired plane, and that's the launch time. \(\sin\gamma_2 = \tan\delta\cot i\) gives the angle along the equator between the ascending node and the launch meridian, and \(\alpha = \Omega + \gamma_2\) tells you where the site must be.`,["node","azimuth"]);
d("lau-133",R`These three headings organize the launch vehicle selection lecture. Capability: can it put your mass into your orbit, quoted as mass to LEO or mass vs C3 for escape. Loads and frequencies: the spacecraft must survive acceleration and vibration and not resonate with the vehicle. Geometry: the stowed spacecraft must fit inside the fairing envelope. Cost, politics and schedule also matter in practice, but they aren't one of the three slide categories.`,["freq","c3"]);
d("lau-134",R`Every launch vehicle has its own vibration frequencies. If the spacecraft's lowest natural frequency is close to one of them, resonance can amplify loads and break things. So user's guides set minimum spacecraft frequencies (the SMAD table lists axial and lateral minimums per vehicle). These formulas treat the spacecraft as a beam: axial stiffness \(AE/L\), lateral bending stiffness \(EI/L^3\). Stiffer or lighter means higher frequency.`,["freq"]);
d("lau-135",R`Within a stage, thrust is roughly constant while mass drops as propellant burns, so acceleration \(F/m\) rises and peaks right before burnout. That's the sawtooth on the 3-stage plot. At staging, acceleration drops sharply because the next stage has a smaller engine pushing a still-heavy stack. Some vehicles throttle down near burnout to cap g-loads (important for crew).`,["staging"]);
d("lau-136",R`Dynamic pressure \(q = \tfrac12\rho V^2\). Early on, \(V\) is small. Higher up, \(\rho\) is tiny. In between, the product peaks, usually about 1 minute into flight at around 10–15 km. That's Max Q, the maximum aerodynamic load on the structure. Vehicles throttle down through it (Case 1 in the staging examples), and throttling all engines equally is not a stage change.`,["drag"]);
d("lau-137",R`From the "Launch Sequence, Vehicles, Considerations" slide: Falcon 9 about 22,800 kg to LEO for about $74M, Falcon Heavy about 63,800 kg for about $97M. Falcon Heavy carries almost 3× the mass for only about 30% more cost, which illustrates the dropping $/kg trend on the $/Mass to LEO slide.`,[]);
d("lau-138",R`The fairing is a light shell that protects the payload from aerodynamic pressure, heating and acoustics on the way up through the atmosphere. Once the vehicle is above most of the air (a few minutes in), it's just dead weight, so it's jettisoned. Dropping it counts as a dry-mass change, one of the staging cases. Its internal envelope sets the maximum size of the stowed spacecraft.`,["staging"]);

/* ---------- Interplanetary ---------- */
d("ipl-139",R`Near Earth, Earth's gravity dominates, so leaving it on an escape trajectory is a hyperbola relative to Earth. Between planets, the Sun dominates, so the cruise is an ellipse around the Sun (a Hohmann for the minimum-energy case). Near the destination, its gravity takes over, so arrival is a hyperbola relative to that planet. Patched conics assumes only one body acts at a time and stitches the three conics together at the SOI boundaries.`,["soi","c3","hohmann"]);
d("ipl-140",R`Energy is conserved along the escape hyperbola. At burnout, \(\varepsilon = v_{bo}^2/2 - \mu/r\). At the SOI edge (effectively infinity), potential energy is about zero, so \(\varepsilon = v_\infty^2/2\). Escape speed at the burnout radius satisfies \(v_{esc}^2/2 = \mu/r\). Substituting gives \(v_\infty^2 = v_{bo}^2 - v_{esc}^2\). Subtracting the speeds directly is wrong: escape "costs" energy, not speed.`,["c3","vcirc"]);
d("ipl-141",R`C3 is twice the specific orbital energy relative to the planet: \(C_3 = 2\varepsilon = v_\infty^2\). It measures how much energy you have left after escaping, so launch vehicles publish "payload mass vs C3" curves (Parker needed 154 km²/s², very high). It is relative to the planet, not the Sun, and since it's a squared speed the units are km²/s².`,["c3"]);
d("ipl-142",R`\(\vec v_\infty\) is your velocity relative to Earth when you leave its SOI. Earth itself moves around the Sun at about 29.8 km/s. In the Sun's frame, add the two as vectors. For an outbound Hohmann (to Mars), you choose the escape geometry so \(\vec v_\infty\) points along Earth's orbital velocity, giving about 29.8 + 2.9 km/s. For Venus, point it backward.`,["vsun","c3"]);
d("ipl-143",R`The SOI is where the planet's pull on the spacecraft (relative to the planet) matters more than the Sun's perturbation. Laplace's analysis gives \(r_{SOI} \approx a(\mu_p/\mu_s)^{2/5}\). For Earth that's about 925,000 km (0.62% of 1 AU). Jupiter is massive and far from the Sun, so its SOI is the largest fraction (6.19%).`,["soi"]);
d("ipl-144",R`Kinetic energy is \(\tfrac12v^2\). Adding \(\Delta v\) at speed \(v\) changes it by \(v\Delta v + \tfrac12\Delta v^2\). The first term grows with \(v\), so the same Δv (same propellant) buys more energy when you're already fast, which is at periapsis. Slide example: 1 m/s at 10 m/s adds 10.5 m²/s², but at 20 m/s it adds 20.5. This is why escape burns happen at perigee and why powered flybys burn at closest approach.`,["oberth"]);
d("ipl-145",R`Start on a parabola (\(v_\infty = 0\)), moving at \(v_{esc}\) at periapsis. Burn \(\Delta v\): energy becomes \(\tfrac12(v_{esc}+\Delta v)^2 - \mu/r = v_{esc}\Delta v + \tfrac12\Delta v^2\). Far away, that's all kinetic, so \(v_\infty = \sqrt{2v_{esc}\Delta v + \Delta v^2}\), which is more than \(\Delta v\). The same burn done far from the planet would only give \(\Delta v\). The planet's gravity well amplifies it.`,["oberth","c3"]);
d("ipl-146",R`You and the target must reach the meeting point at the same time. During the transfer time, the target moves \(2\pi t_{xfer}/T_2\) around its orbit. You sweep 180° (Hohmann). So at departure the target must already be ahead by \(180° - 360°\,t_{xfer}/T_2\). For Mars, \(t_{xfer} \approx 259\) days and \(T_2 = 687\), so Mars leads by about 44°. HW 2 #2d does the same thing for the Moon.`,["phase","hohmann"]);
d("ipl-147",R`The synodic period is how long until the same Earth–target alignment repeats. Each planet moves at its own angular rate, and the relative angle changes at \(2\pi/T_1 - 2\pi/T_2\). One full relative lap takes \(T_1T_2/|T_2-T_1|\). For Mars that's about 780 days, which is why Mars launch windows open every 26 months.`,["synodic"]);
d("ipl-148",R`The type refers to the transfer angle, how far the spacecraft goes around the Sun between departure and arrival. Type I is under 180° (a short way), Type II is over 180° (a long way). Near exactly 180°, the departure and arrival points are opposite, so the transfer plane is set by the Sun and the planets' tiny inclinations, forcing a large plane change. That's the costly "ridge" between the two lobes of the porkchop.`,[]);
d("ipl-149",R`Each point on a porkchop plot is a Lambert solution for one departure date and one arrival date. Contours show the C3 needed at Earth (and \(v_\infty\) at arrival). The two "chops" are the Type I and Type II families. Mission designers use them to find the cheapest dates, trade C3 against flight time (e.g., the human mission's 200-day limit in HW 4), and read off launch windows.`,["c3"]);
d("ipl-150",R`Draw the C3 contour equal to what your launch vehicle can deliver (21 km²/s² here). All departure dates where the required C3 is at or below that contour, for acceptable arrival dates, form the launch window. On the 2005 example that's about 23–24 days for each transfer type. A bigger launch vehicle (higher C3) widens the window.`,["c3"]);
d("ipl-151",R`In the planet's frame, a flyby is just a hyperbola: you arrive at \(v_\infty\) and leave at the same \(v_\infty\), only pointing a different direction (energy is conserved relative to the planet). In the Sun's frame, you add the planet's velocity before and after. Because the direction changed, the sum changes, so your heliocentric speed can increase or decrease with no propellant. The planet loses or gains a negligible amount of momentum.`,["assist","vsun"]);
d("ipl-152",R`Subway map legs, Earth surface to Mars orbit: 9.4 km/s to LEO, 3.21 to escape Earth, 1.06 heliocentric to reach Mars, 1.44 to capture at Mars (≈ 15.1 km/s total). The 1.44 km/s leg is the one HW 4 #4 uses for Mars orbit insertion. Subway maps skip details like plane changes and launch losses, but they give quick budget estimates.`,["c3","hohmann"]);
d("ipl-153",R`Normal orbit propagation goes: given position and velocity now, find position later. Lambert's problem runs it the other way: given two positions and the time between them, find the velocities that connect them. Solving it for every departure/arrival date pair, then turning the departure velocity into C3, is how porkchop plots are built.`,["c3"]);
d("ipl-154",R`With three bodies, the motion is generally chaotic and has no closed-form solution, so it's solved numerically. Lagrange points are special spots where gravity and the rotating frame's centrifugal effect balance, so a spacecraft can stay fixed relative to the two big bodies. L1, L2 and L3 lie on the line through them and are unstable (they need stationkeeping). L4 and L5 sit 60° ahead and behind and are stable. JWST orbits Sun–Earth L2, about 1.5 million km from Earth.`,[]);
d("ipl-155",R`An orbiter uses a capture burn to turn the arrival hyperbola into an orbit. A lander uses the atmosphere instead. Entry: an aeroshell and heat shield slow the vehicle from km/s speeds. Descent: a parachute slows it further, then retrorockets. Landing: airbags (MER), legs, or a sky crane (MSL). MER also used a bridle and RAD rockets just before airbag impact.`,["c3"]);
d("ipl-156",R`On the porkchop plot, required C3 depends on departure date. The window has a minimum-C3 date, and C3 rises on either side. Rover A launched near the cheap part of the 2003 window. Rover B launched about a month later, when more energy was needed to reach Mars on schedule. So it flew on the uprated 7925-H (Heavy), with bigger strap-on boosters.`,["c3"]);
d("ipl-157",R`About 16,000 spacecraft are active around Earth (about 15,000 of them in LEO, largely Starlink), against about 130 cis-lunar and about 100 that have gone beyond the Moon. So only about 1% have ever left Earth orbit. That rarity reflects the cost of the extra Δv and the complexity of interplanetary missions.`,[]);
d("ipl-158",R`Even Earth's SOI (about 925,000 km) is tiny next to its 150-million-km orbit. So when the spacecraft crosses the SOI boundary, patched conics treats its position as the planet's position. Its velocity, though, is the planet's plus \(\vec v_\infty\), which is exactly what sets up the heliocentric transfer.`,["soi","vsun"]);

/* ---------- Missions ---------- */
d("mis-159",R`In 1964, Gary Flandro, then a JPL summer intern, noticed that Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus and Neptune would line up in the late 1970s (about once every 175 years). A spacecraft could then use each planet's gravity to fly on to the next. Voyager 2 did the full tour, and both Voyagers are now in interstellar space, farther than any other human-made object.`,["assist"]);
d("mis-160",R`Galileo (launched 1989) used Venus and Earth gravity assists to reach Jupiter. Its large high-gain antenna jammed and never fully opened, likely from lubricant lost or cold-welded pins after years in storage. The team rebuilt the mission around the low-gain antenna with heavy data compression, at 10–20 bps instead of 134.4 kbps. It's the course's example of why mechanisms are mission-critical and hard to test in 1 g.`,[]);
d("mis-161",R`Reaction wheels give Hubble fine, smooth pointing, but external torques build up momentum in the wheels until they saturate. Instead of thrusters (which would contaminate the optics), magnetic torquers push against Earth's magnetic field to dump that momentum. Gyros measure rotation rates for precise control. It can slew 90° in under 18 minutes.`,[]);
d("mis-162",R`At Sun–Earth L2, about 1.5 million km beyond Earth, the Sun, Earth and Moon are always on the same side. One sunshield can block all three, keeping JWST's infrared optics around 40 K. L2 is unstable, so JWST flies a halo orbit around it and does small stationkeeping burns.`,[]);
d("mis-163",R`DART deliberately crashed into Dimorphos, a small moon orbiting the asteroid Didymos, to test kinetic-impact asteroid deflection. Because Dimorphos orbits Didymos, the change in its orbital period could be measured from Earth. The course lists it as near-Earth, with critical targeting (an autonomous camera guided the final approach) and science observation (LICIACube imaged the impact, and Hubble watched the debris tail).`,[]);
d("mis-164",R`To reach the Sun, a spacecraft has to shed most of Earth's 30 km/s orbital speed, far too much to do with propellant. Parker used a very high-C3 launch (154 km²/s²) and then seven Venus flybys, each removing heliocentric energy and lowering perihelion. The final orbits bring it to about 9.86 solar radii, inside the corona.`,["assist","c3"]);
d("mis-165",R`Most missions park in LEO first. MMX's launch vehicle puts it directly onto an Earth-escape hyperbola (positive C3), so it heads to Mars right away. JAXA's goal is to land on Phobos, collect about 10 g of regolith, and return it to Earth (targeted for 2031) to learn whether the Martian moons are captured asteroids or debris from an impact on Mars.`,["c3"]);
d("mis-166",R`Cassini was too heavy to go straight to Saturn, so it flew Venus–Venus–Earth–Jupiter gravity assists (VVEJGA) to build up energy. At Saturn, Titan, the large moon in a very regular orbit, served as a free engine. Each Titan flyby could change Cassini's velocity by more than 800 m/s, letting the team reshape the orbit hundreds of times over 13 years with limited propellant.`,["assist"]);
d("mis-167",R`Enceladus has a subsurface ocean venting into space, and Titan has complex organic chemistry. Both might host life or its precursors. A dead, uncontrolled spacecraft could eventually crash into one and contaminate it with Earth microbes (planetary protection). So the team used one last Titan flyby to aim Cassini into Saturn, collecting data on the way down in the "Grand Finale."`,[]);
d("mis-168",R`These missions need large total Δv over long times, which is where EP's high Isp wins. DAWN used ion engines to orbit both Vesta and Ceres. Starlink uses Hall thrusters for orbit raising and stationkeeping. Gateway's Power and Propulsion Element uses high-power Hall thrusters. ARM was a planned asteroid-capture mission. DART also carried an ion drive (NEXT-C).`,[]);
d("mis-169",R`About 3,132 kg at launch: 3,000 kg of biprop for big maneuvers (like Saturn orbit insertion) and 132 kg of monoprop for small trims and attitude. That's more than the spacecraft's 2,113 kg dry mass. Even with all the Titan flybys, Cassini used most of it, and about 80% was gone by the end of the first extended mission.`,[]);

window.AAE_DEEP = D;
})();
